  "summary": "Four preprints tie climate, aridity, and seasonal gradients to morphological and microbial variation across birds, marsupials, lemurs, and seabirds.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the association between environmental gradients (thermal conditions, aridity, seasonal rainfall and temperature) and organismal variation across taxa. Across 5,715 bird species in 513 genera, thermal similarity among congeners best explains variation in the strength of Bergmann's rule. In 30 Sminthopsinae marsupial species, relative auditory bulla size rises about 20% along an aridity gradient, which the authors interpret as skull adaptation to arid environments. A 10-month study of red-fronted brown lemurs in Ranomafana links seasonal rainfall, temperature and diet to shifts in gut microbial communities. A seabird study reports that individual foraging-effort response diversity correlates positively with colony population stability in two North Atlantic species. What is new across these preprints is quantitative linkage of environmental variables to traits at the species, individual, or community level, not a shared mechanism. The items establish correlations, and they do not establish causation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
